================================================================================
4. FIXED, SAME DAY: THE IDS ARE NO LONGER POSITIONS
================================================================================

Point 2 is closed at the source rather than only made detectable.

pf_dedup() now derives each identity's id from the identity's own content: the
lexicographically smallest EXACT dedup key in its cluster, hashed, as
PFI-<16 hex> (pf_identity_id()). Exact keys partition the rows, so each belongs
to exactly one identity and the minimum is canonical -- independent of row
order, of which uid a fuzzy component happened to take, and of how many other
identities exist. Rows with neither name nor address have no content to hash;
they are keyed on OBJECTID and marked PFX-, and are always out of scope.

What that buys is not merely stability. It is that a collision with a different
organisation becomes impossible: if the id matches, the key matches, so the
organisation matches by construction. An identity whose key set really did
change gets a different id, and the old label is then VISIBLY ABSENT rather
than silently re-attached. Absent is recoverable; wrong is not.

  recipient_id   the stable id; what the cascade and every label key on
  recipient_key  the canonical key it hashes, kept so an id can be re-derived
  recipient_uid  the old integer, kept for cheap within-run grouping only

pf_recipients() keys the cascade on recipient_id (and warns if it has to fall
back), pf_propagate_matches() joins on whichever key the status frame was built
with and stops rather than joining nothing, pf_queue() prices on it, and
pf_read_truth() REFUSES a label carrying an R<k> id -- the shape of the id is
the last place the hazard is visible, and the refusal lands before the labels
are graded rather than after.

TRI-V2-MATCH.rds was rekeyed in place, with no rematch: the id is a function of
the exact keys, and every grant row of every identity is in the artifact's own
`grants` frame. dev/rekey-tri-v2.R recomputes them through .pf_exact_keys() and
.pf_identity_keys() -- the same functions pf_dedup() calls, not a copy, because
a reimplementation that drifted by one character would hand out ids that look
right and name other organisations. It checks the installed normalizer against
the frame's run stamp first, since the keys are normalizer-derived.

  596,539 grant rows -> 91,134 identity ids, 0 not content-identified
  map one-to-one: 91,134 old ids -> 91,134 new, nothing merged
  all 91,008 status identities found
  new id lands on a row carrying the same name: 91,008 of 91,008
  frame_id 72edafa8f2114af9 -> 89a2a597ed0ea128

Stage 1 was then redrawn and picked THE SAME 400 identities -- verified by
mapping the previous draw's ids through the frame's own uid-to-id
correspondence and comparing the sets. Only the keys changed, which is the
result worth having: the round is unchanged and now re-attachable.

Dealt into 8 shards of 50 primaries, 40 blinded overlap cases (10%), seed
20261103, key written to data-dev/round-stage1-key/ rather than beside the
shards. The case files carry the identity and the cascade's pick, the latter
because TRUTH-LABELING-PROTOCOL section 4 asks for `wrong_ein` "especially when
